my name is Andrew cuenta men and I'm an  assistant professor of Religious Studies  in the Department of Religious Studies  at Yale University and I work on the  Buddhist traditions of Tibet and  Himalayas we're here today in the by  Nicki Rare Book and Manuscript library  it is a place that has a very wonderful  collection of Tibetan and Himalayan  woodblock prints and manuscript texts  but they also have a really wonderful  collection of portable Tibetan religious  scroll paintings called Tong codes so  we'll be looking at some of those  paintings here today tanka is a Tibetan  portable religious painting it's a  scroll painting so it could be rolled up  and easily transported subject matters  are almost always religious in nature  Tonka painting could be commissioned by  high Lama for ritual purposes or it  could be commissioned by an ordinary  family in the event of a death in the  family for another kind of religious  purpose so painting our religious  painting was a great act of Merit making  in in traditional Buddhist societies  Tibetan tanka paintings vary greatly in  terms of subject matter in style  iconography and the kinds of figures  that they depict but there are certain  key elements that are common to almost  all Tibetan tanka paintings so first the  central painting would be prepared on  canvas canvas was usually white cotton  that was framed in a stretcher a kind of  gesso was applied to it then the painter  would usually lay out the econometrica  so the kind of framework of grid lines  and the outline of the central figures  the deities or the Buddha those lines  would then be overlaid with paint or ink  so the outlining was done first and then  gradually the colors would be added  usually first the background and then  the details of the figure and then  finally the face would be done the eyes  and the guild work so most tanka  paintings actually have a kind of liquid  gold that's applied to them but once the  painting was finished that was really  only the first part of the  struction of a Tonka painting the canvas  would be taken off of its stretcher and  then would be set within a complex  brocade frame so most Tonga's like this  one have an interior framework with  several kinds of alternating color broke  aids and then that was set within a  larger brocade frame and then at the top  and the bottom they usually had wood  bars that would help to stretch the  Tonka so that it would lay flat while  hanging on the arm and you can see on  the bottom here there are ornamental  ends that were usually placed on the  wooden bar and then finally there was a  curtain that was attached to the top of  the Tonka that could be raised up when  the tonk I needed to be displayed either  for ritual ceremonies or even if it was  just being left open in a shrine but if  the painting needed to be transported or  protected in some way then the curtain  could be lowered and that would protect  the painting from the elements from  sunlight or dirt or dust or things like  that so Tibetan painting is generally  rigidly defined traditional Tibetan  painters had a little leeway in terms of  korea expressing their own kind of  creativity so what this means is that  the detailed figures the deities the  buddha figures had proportions that were  clearly delineated within texts and so  one of the painters first tasks would be  to sort of translate the econometrics  that were transmitted in texts into  visual form and so every figure  including the figure of a Buddha that  you see here would have its proportions  sort of rigidly laid out and so a  Tibetan painter would lay out a series  of grid lines that would determine the  proportion of the face to the body and  the body torso to the overall figure it  would guide the painter and where to  place the eyes or other important Akeno  graphic features of a Buddha such as the  cranial bump called the huge nisha on  his head there were also strict  descriptions of the kinds of hand  gestures called mudras or the physical  pop body postures or like postures  called asanas these were also clearly  laid  out in texts so once the econometrics  for the central figure was laid out and  the secondary figures were late were  laid out then the painter had some  flexibility in terms of how he laid out  the background and that could in some  instances include kind of narrative  stories like we see in this painting  here or it could include kind of  environmental features things like  mountains or streams or sky and things  like that and that was really the realm  in which the artist had great license to  express his own creativity so this  painting was a gift to the by Nicki  library from Edna Breyer schwab in 1967  and it was probably part of a set we  have at least one other painting that  was part of this set and the central  figure is the historical buddha  shakyamuni buddha and the scenes around  it described a collection of his past  lives called Jatakas now the Jatakas  were a really important form of Buddhist  literature they are found in Buddhist  traditions across Asia in the Pali Canon  in the Tibetan and Chinese cannons as  well there were hundreds of different  Jataka stories and there were several  which were really important in Tibet and  this is one of those stories so the  central image here is the historical  buddha shakyamuni and he's shown in a  kind of traditional form he seated  cross-legged on a lotus throne and the  lotus is supported by a throne held up  by force no lines very traditional  Tibetan motif you'll see that he sitted  with crossed legs and his left hand is  held in meditation posture it holds a  begging bowl here looks like it's filled  with flowers in his right hand is held  in the mudra of touching the earth  called a boomy sparsha mudra and this is  a representation of the moment of the  Buddha's enlightenment and surrounding  the central figure then is this great  narrative from the story of the Buddha's  previous life as Prince vasant era so  the judge ago stories were were very  important  form of Buddhist literature that  described the Buddha's past lives they  were often written in the form of  morality tales which describe a  particular kind of virtue that the  Buddha had to practice in a past life in  order to eventually become the Buddha  himself and the Buddha the center story  then was one of the most important of  these Jatakas stories in Tibet where it  was not only recorded in texts but it  was also performed in stage plays and  was widely widely beloved across to bed
 

